[{:db/id "q-161908/selection/c8342a70c8e62051c47712f0d2fb90e2cca2ae41fa32ebd616f49ff0ee9032f1" :answer/id #uuid "5b293e36-7131-5414-90be-87ddf25eb079" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161908/selection/b70caf718087bb0020d5d5a4f9bc319fc74a954b52b99a75849c3ff5f7476285" :answer/id #uuid "af99e1ee-a46c-5d57-9f3d-5346f2ea9bd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161908/selection/82826f8ecb5cbd70a7c95d58d98c89eaec6861a1e10b4d42d34ed3441476a115" :answer/id #uuid "0f36fb53-30eb-5eeb-a5cb-d2243a9a5cbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161908/selection/fbff51082f68235b416393ac6b32c66c5e132ddfcfd5743992626770a2a66de8" :answer/id #uuid "bf7f910c-2c8d-5dbe-9358-5018562e4abc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161908/selection/e843eb0d4019f921f2a694c70fa3f32d2998d22af7d5c44a599c49901744afd1" :answer/id #uuid "0613470b-ec84-546e-86ca-38676c9a8e94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161908/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce3239f-6733-58fe-b720-d388a67a1827" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161908/selection/e843eb0d4019f921f2a694c70fa3f32d2998d22af7d5c44a599c49901744afd1" :answer-field/choices ["q-161908/selection/c8342a70c8e62051c47712f0d2fb90e2cca2ae41fa32ebd616f49ff0ee9032f1" "q-161908/selection/b70caf718087bb0020d5d5a4f9bc319fc74a954b52b99a75849c3ff5f7476285" "q-161908/selection/82826f8ecb5cbd70a7c95d58d98c89eaec6861a1e10b4d42d34ed3441476a115" "q-161908/selection/fbff51082f68235b416393ac6b32c66c5e132ddfcfd5743992626770a2a66de8" "q-161908/selection/e843eb0d4019f921f2a694c70fa3f32d2998d22af7d5c44a599c49901744afd1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161908" :question/id #uuid "883193fd-f5be-5d1c-8ffa-32b09d1431cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161908" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/2fff2eab1f5670dfcd3351dcbb9be1b3110e6ebdcd452cf4c5a273611d454c8b.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161908/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161908"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161910/selection/2bb0e3a8cd8bbf71f6cb6a68866632ba6b7fff4b9f2d14305150502d78bfb386" :answer/id #uuid "eee982ac-cfca-5d03-a57c-278b2d139100" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xy-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161910/selection/7ef607c6ab15f51751e3eb3c35c08b0887937ae5fbf6e1afe673f59ccafe668c" :answer/id #uuid "6c8e1246-763b-5440-86da-c1ebeb83a284" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161910/selection/adfa6bb4fe55a4acfd1c161003157229f7c684ab60887d398c683d8a8fb1dfb0" :answer/id #uuid "b17abe20-b1f2-5202-8251-02dcefc35c41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161910/selection/4c87c80ab078cfaf8278c89b26660b855aeaeb1f31a299c904f43b72ab11813b" :answer/id #uuid "d15df8d0-78cb-5542-9a8e-1d2392044cff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161910/selection/f4f7505e03c7d5fd0ad48472923a0920171323d297169e4502fde44b8f25cec6" :answer/id #uuid "b8f88975-d93a-54bb-893b-88f1106c897f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+(x+y)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161910/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c36c108b-f740-54fa-805a-1ad869b6e112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161910/selection/4c87c80ab078cfaf8278c89b26660b855aeaeb1f31a299c904f43b72ab11813b" :answer-field/choices ["q-161910/selection/2bb0e3a8cd8bbf71f6cb6a68866632ba6b7fff4b9f2d14305150502d78bfb386" "q-161910/selection/7ef607c6ab15f51751e3eb3c35c08b0887937ae5fbf6e1afe673f59ccafe668c" "q-161910/selection/adfa6bb4fe55a4acfd1c161003157229f7c684ab60887d398c683d8a8fb1dfb0" "q-161910/selection/4c87c80ab078cfaf8278c89b26660b855aeaeb1f31a299c904f43b72ab11813b" "q-161910/selection/f4f7505e03c7d5fd0ad48472923a0920171323d297169e4502fde44b8f25cec6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161910" :question/id #uuid "39e1c3e2-6428-5a2a-8b1d-95017ac2ef6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161910" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/01779de50ae00cf2883bee0e021638a423989b87a69aaa17e0332c4a671a32ca.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-y,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-y,z,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y+\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+{∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{xy+4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161910/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161910"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161901/selection/bdb55ad0f6473f62ba59d76a4871960e4310fa80b386ac52e6bc46351dd153cd" :answer/id #uuid "a54e41be-21f7-50c0-add8-402f24c9cfc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161901/selection/a31764af807fc7967786a140d52ac60dc7f8411601bfc2aa570f83859db71038" :answer/id #uuid "d7a30733-8953-5156-8058-2567c5d9c088" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161901/selection/a7e87dc5e5ec99234a6e5b6b852a1a63668d1f1b067e3bf8755a93c3cee83b5e" :answer/id #uuid "e4135d67-f437-5326-82c8-5bfabe55338c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161901/selection/4d70a98975aede469ff3006f070b741be1ae47e7c1405fb98e38e4ba378b5492" :answer/id #uuid "c3d5db3a-9b49-57d1-9674-7f7f712bd5b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161901/selection/23d62e8b0bb92fbc70b73e51dac8de5a36c180d9b08d3bd954b7284577e62e79" :answer/id #uuid "1c498de6-9a5f-5702-9de8-add8ffe1b816" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161901/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9423072b-912c-5bf1-beea-06b077cc7f29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161901/selection/23d62e8b0bb92fbc70b73e51dac8de5a36c180d9b08d3bd954b7284577e62e79" :answer-field/choices ["q-161901/selection/bdb55ad0f6473f62ba59d76a4871960e4310fa80b386ac52e6bc46351dd153cd" "q-161901/selection/a31764af807fc7967786a140d52ac60dc7f8411601bfc2aa570f83859db71038" "q-161901/selection/a7e87dc5e5ec99234a6e5b6b852a1a63668d1f1b067e3bf8755a93c3cee83b5e" "q-161901/selection/4d70a98975aede469ff3006f070b741be1ae47e7c1405fb98e38e4ba378b5492" "q-161901/selection/23d62e8b0bb92fbc70b73e51dac8de5a36c180d9b08d3bd954b7284577e62e79"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161901" :question/id #uuid "e3eea45a-e598-5192-b757-2e1b9a1153d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161901" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/c7f140ad2c5687db48171819e1efaa858701af881ebbee57ff835da569f30947.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨{z}^{2},0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161901/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161901"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161895/selection/585c2cfc44c292b514a133ccbec463a22aeea64062d9ff305f3f2d2a3a09ab41" :answer/id #uuid "04b66e8b-f9a7-5730-b513-8a9797591ab6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161895/selection/312c672f61fda53732f65b9a101db6c881a75fe5004e069237a71ac6aef50ec0" :answer/id #uuid "d30fd415-bef5-5bad-854f-586531d53c94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+10x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161895/selection/47e0767e334753b5fc4e98f2b6b8531411f5d0ef8cb30dd44ae068b4de896248" :answer/id #uuid "e426fb22-8e31-596a-a5a4-0b9ed2a693f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-6x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161895/selection/389f7fcb9250b03ad9367c6cf691c7e2fda18a2febf99c2edc332b20934ac428" :answer/id #uuid "8cba9584-7c2c-51c1-8955-b32bb09d86b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161895/selection/68f91d0d06bc73a178b1858753753449801132bd08f19c4d2315c1a8cb64a003" :answer/id #uuid "eb1abf14-3421-564c-8532-63312b865da5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161895/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf521ea-fc63-5207-ab84-eb3a3c45490f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161895/selection/68f91d0d06bc73a178b1858753753449801132bd08f19c4d2315c1a8cb64a003" :answer-field/choices ["q-161895/selection/585c2cfc44c292b514a133ccbec463a22aeea64062d9ff305f3f2d2a3a09ab41" "q-161895/selection/312c672f61fda53732f65b9a101db6c881a75fe5004e069237a71ac6aef50ec0" "q-161895/selection/47e0767e334753b5fc4e98f2b6b8531411f5d0ef8cb30dd44ae068b4de896248" "q-161895/selection/389f7fcb9250b03ad9367c6cf691c7e2fda18a2febf99c2edc332b20934ac428" "q-161895/selection/68f91d0d06bc73a178b1858753753449801132bd08f19c4d2315c1a8cb64a003"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161895" :question/id #uuid "87242b70-612b-5db0-9c6f-2022ddd8e964" :question/math-academy-id "q-161895" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/0e374f18d8b87de2fddd9a2127ee84709746c7c1b75083441afcc7b6c79402fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-z,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161895/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161895"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161911/selection/58b6800473224040cf4067c265e29b309363fecda9276e7cb32c68ca5de5761b" :answer/id #uuid "8ada3790-56c2-531a-abe5-5fb085bee02e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4x{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+2x-6y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161911/selection/e9ad8c009553a9b0e202e3d788b0d7592617ab3493f5eecde0e22b1bb48de246" :answer/id #uuid "8ef4f59d-12ba-50a5-bbdb-0e4f79061f78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2x+6y-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161911/selection/29e1fa8084962a9b2d0209d4b5b661a356bac4bc1a7fff11d1f0b33ad692e966" :answer/id #uuid "b772ee87-d705-5659-b051-efc94d4c90c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161911/selection/2e4ef775cc19ecfe735350167aab60749ad74c503459352c4ef0544cbd3bdfc1" :answer/id #uuid "29d7259e-0555-5c91-8a3e-b9884803f7f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2xy-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161911/selection/f154df6bd87d506c5ea60fc039dd79e062039d4929dc54dc4bd6b8db34b9e7a0" :answer/id #uuid "f2f824a0-4b51-572c-bdd2-e6e4a8b8394c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2{y}^{3}-12xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161911/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f52623e9-5877-5e25-a2a5-c27c07ea810f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161911/selection/29e1fa8084962a9b2d0209d4b5b661a356bac4bc1a7fff11d1f0b33ad692e966" :answer-field/choices ["q-161911/selection/58b6800473224040cf4067c265e29b309363fecda9276e7cb32c68ca5de5761b" "q-161911/selection/e9ad8c009553a9b0e202e3d788b0d7592617ab3493f5eecde0e22b1bb48de246" "q-161911/selection/29e1fa8084962a9b2d0209d4b5b661a356bac4bc1a7fff11d1f0b33ad692e966" "q-161911/selection/2e4ef775cc19ecfe735350167aab60749ad74c503459352c4ef0544cbd3bdfc1" "q-161911/selection/f154df6bd87d506c5ea60fc039dd79e062039d4929dc54dc4bd6b8db34b9e7a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161911" :question/id #uuid "3d156036-efde-59a7-b837-c977a3c50b6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161911" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/5709af87720dcb052d8ffc6cc600c5889e45849aee6bed69ec3646e43c3cefb1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,1,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3,\\,Q=1,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)\\cdot 2x-1\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161911/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161911"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161904/selection/557bb2d912ba1f8092f63aa026dfee60c9ec4a1268d4063410a8aa069545385c" :answer/id #uuid "6712318d-b9ee-5f22-a548-42e5f6083ab2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x-y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161904/selection/964eea9446770c849581128c5843dcfee6f465a8454fa968f440cf8ae42a6a53" :answer/id #uuid "83a82167-a9b9-5ca0-b834-d0d12fe1b1f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161904/selection/999690e439e40b6cdf26f3d70b3362dcfe183a1d593133837a84a1a4c9defeee" :answer/id #uuid "08045a6e-6387-5980-b29e-b3ac005cf271" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161904/selection/ce09173e4b383d8653fbf54e49db07fea7c8181dcd788913f3cf591a66df4c2b" :answer/id #uuid "0fc97b4b-78dc-59ca-a5c7-dbec2ed100c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+2y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161904/selection/4ef11d2b7b8d4b157c0659f12e43c0a543e1a8cc2daea15ddb741210163b6c8f" :answer/id #uuid "192c4460-e346-5161-a9f9-ef6c54ae210d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161904/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "025aa2b3-25c3-5a10-ae18-f680a03c33fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161904/selection/4ef11d2b7b8d4b157c0659f12e43c0a543e1a8cc2daea15ddb741210163b6c8f" :answer-field/choices ["q-161904/selection/557bb2d912ba1f8092f63aa026dfee60c9ec4a1268d4063410a8aa069545385c" "q-161904/selection/964eea9446770c849581128c5843dcfee6f465a8454fa968f440cf8ae42a6a53" "q-161904/selection/999690e439e40b6cdf26f3d70b3362dcfe183a1d593133837a84a1a4c9defeee" "q-161904/selection/ce09173e4b383d8653fbf54e49db07fea7c8181dcd788913f3cf591a66df4c2b" "q-161904/selection/4ef11d2b7b8d4b157c0659f12e43c0a543e1a8cc2daea15ddb741210163b6c8f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161904" :question/id #uuid "150a753b-c416-5943-8cfa-2d89a5b17df4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161904" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨-y,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-y,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-(0)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161904/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161904"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161905/selection/68f91d0d06bc73a178b1858753753449801132bd08f19c4d2315c1a8cb64a003" :answer/id #uuid "0321e38e-1c77-54cb-9cbd-f9ac0526471e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161905/selection/a76e64dd0406ade29087c59ec1aac41b3638f8df3eab8b5aad75e54b9f6b4cb0" :answer/id #uuid "6467024a-608e-543a-b155-2fc0237d33db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-2y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161905/selection/e31d1944c56b8da7169e2ebf6e62afd5bda3878f0eb797e4c3ebc0fc31a79e90" :answer/id #uuid "db0dea77-fa80-5800-a581-315c42c62676" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}+4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161905/selection/8d01e6a511b17d77d72736d84a41bd6f3e578000843373ac533a274b64cf37d1" :answer/id #uuid "b91ee57c-77ce-51e2-b8cc-40f5fc7419c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-5x+y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161905/selection/81594993e0999f6ddc93c49ee279f28366b8a228ec9a91a52b8f38babfb5417b" :answer/id #uuid "5fe1a8c2-8abc-5140-af1e-95aff8bb495a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161905/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0946e5a6-7068-5678-be77-e55ddaa8ce9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161905/selection/68f91d0d06bc73a178b1858753753449801132bd08f19c4d2315c1a8cb64a003" :answer-field/choices ["q-161905/selection/68f91d0d06bc73a178b1858753753449801132bd08f19c4d2315c1a8cb64a003" "q-161905/selection/a76e64dd0406ade29087c59ec1aac41b3638f8df3eab8b5aad75e54b9f6b4cb0" "q-161905/selection/e31d1944c56b8da7169e2ebf6e62afd5bda3878f0eb797e4c3ebc0fc31a79e90" "q-161905/selection/8d01e6a511b17d77d72736d84a41bd6f3e578000843373ac533a274b64cf37d1" "q-161905/selection/81594993e0999f6ddc93c49ee279f28366b8a228ec9a91a52b8f38babfb5417b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161905" :question/id #uuid "64b8ff17-b4b5-5df6-a8c6-0e2b7a0379fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161905" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161905/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161905"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161913/selection/5a7c8c6ca9a2d0c7368b4ab28d9ee72b0ad8dc6fa7e00e509cdef67041d4924c" :answer/id #uuid "ba2bec9d-e1f2-5711-a943-9b3f687ebb78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161913/selection/fc80c17398065b660a228cad48b22af155be7c6739bb6a728a1b795eaa47b2df" :answer/id #uuid "6703398b-5dd0-5e2d-b55b-77476519b427" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161913/selection/f66c1fc8a0f7c5f78f403ff74cddb7af549bfea70a115cadbc41c421591335a1" :answer/id #uuid "c8ec492f-d185-5d74-952d-0227ebfb4d79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161913/selection/72df5251cc5107c08be1aa4f9ce08f1a0d24f10f9401abf5dc0f8f6ac91def26" :answer/id #uuid "251659f2-bb76-5360-a291-a30930c23c69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161913/selection/b94020f5be39b672763abdb52d864836008480beacf29599496658e91d04317f" :answer/id #uuid "31015939-d6d7-5b08-ba18-04bc236d7cd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161913/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "15efbc2f-4a60-5878-b4a6-cb1313a0bf80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161913/selection/fc80c17398065b660a228cad48b22af155be7c6739bb6a728a1b795eaa47b2df" :answer-field/choices ["q-161913/selection/5a7c8c6ca9a2d0c7368b4ab28d9ee72b0ad8dc6fa7e00e509cdef67041d4924c" "q-161913/selection/fc80c17398065b660a228cad48b22af155be7c6739bb6a728a1b795eaa47b2df" "q-161913/selection/f66c1fc8a0f7c5f78f403ff74cddb7af549bfea70a115cadbc41c421591335a1" "q-161913/selection/72df5251cc5107c08be1aa4f9ce08f1a0d24f10f9401abf5dc0f8f6ac91def26" "q-161913/selection/b94020f5be39b672763abdb52d864836008480beacf29599496658e91d04317f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161913" :question/id #uuid "7b5db020-d92f-50c2-8c6b-3f44b9d6b975" :question/math-academy-id "q-161913" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the parabolic cylinder $z={y}^{2}-2.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={y}^{2}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(0)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cylinder ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-2)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}={({y}^{2}-2)}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-{({y}^{2}-2)}^{2}\\cdot (0)+0\\cdot (2y)+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161913/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161913"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-161907"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=1+x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1+x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 2x-0\\cdot 2y+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x-1\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-161914/selection/28553be23bb7ab86abc27cd6b0b61b4550a1824fdf9a8b56f77bcdb341555ebf" :answer/id #uuid "c3c826f0-9a32-5c2b-9a29-0e956dd2ce77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2y{x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}-y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161914/selection/257d8767ef03965f86b445e37b9e6ca54f2f095dacaa2c32f7a06759341b7688" :answer/id #uuid "d69f35c9-8b2f-52b5-98d4-a2d94aacc39f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2y{x}^{2}-y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161914/selection/0c9c0661b0f3187ae5b1c566d1a0a9616e666a80106f6d66418fccd3b2466a3a" :answer/id #uuid "80d7b34b-9462-548f-bf17-08292ef47d3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{y}^{3}+y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161914/selection/e0a5638f37c0f06e0f686818d06ab23e89bd6cb4488d62176fe0cea2e4314268" :answer/id #uuid "8876fb0a-3f4c-5478-9932-e5456974cca5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161914/selection/5804101bf90f679d672f05e3459ff3209e883b11f3b2c8adc0843ee64c023927" :answer/id #uuid "918a6ff8-3a90-53dc-b1fe-a8d9cf507b03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-3y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161914/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9962cdec-7208-5071-8ab5-9181c1a293ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161914/selection/e0a5638f37c0f06e0f686818d06ab23e89bd6cb4488d62176fe0cea2e4314268" :answer-field/choices ["q-161914/selection/28553be23bb7ab86abc27cd6b0b61b4550a1824fdf9a8b56f77bcdb341555ebf" "q-161914/selection/257d8767ef03965f86b445e37b9e6ca54f2f095dacaa2c32f7a06759341b7688" "q-161914/selection/0c9c0661b0f3187ae5b1c566d1a0a9616e666a80106f6d66418fccd3b2466a3a" "q-161914/selection/e0a5638f37c0f06e0f686818d06ab23e89bd6cb4488d62176fe0cea2e4314268" "q-161914/selection/5804101bf90f679d672f05e3459ff3209e883b11f3b2c8adc0843ee64c023927"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161914" :question/id #uuid "91499b2a-f503-536e-9f6f-5cde9bd57186" :question/math-academy-id "q-161914" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0+y+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (2x)-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(0-2y{x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2y{x}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161914/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161914"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12419"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073335/assets/263853df5f080da778a34caacea945119099326f0ba27806d851f793416e9f92.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 0, \\, x, \\, -z^2 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nDetermine the missing expression." :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = -\\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane $S_1.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,x,-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere $S_2.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z = h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = 0, \\qquad\nQ = x, \\qquad\nR = -z^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 4.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12420"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\, y, \\, 0 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinder $z=1-x^2$ and the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z=g(x,y)=1-x^2$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z=h(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper parabolic cylinder $S_1.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y, \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (-2x)-y\\cdot (0)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid $S_2.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = y , \\qquad\nR = 0.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot (2x)-y\\cdot (2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
